{"name":"Water Wheel","hex":"#a28566","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"water wheel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A wheel, propelled by running or falling water, used to power machinery.","A wheel with buckets used to raise water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/water_wheel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Water wheel","blocks":[{"text":"A water wheel is a machine for converting the kinetic energy of flowing or falling water into useful forms of power, often in a watermill. A water wheel consists of a large wheel, with numerous blades or buckets attached to the outer rim forming the drive mechanism. Water wheels were still in commercial use well into the 20th century, although they are no longer in common use today. Water wheels are used for milling flour in gristmills, grinding wood into pulp for papermaking, hammering wrought iron, machining, ore crushing and pounding fibre for use in the manufacture of cloth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Brucker_Wasserrad_01.webm/330px--Brucker_Wasserrad_01.webm.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_wheel","wikidata":"Q146728"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water","wikidata":"Q283"},{"title":"Wheel","blocks":[{"text":"A wheel is a rotating component intended to turn on an axle bearing. The wheel is one of the key components of the wheel and axle, which is one of the six simple machines. Wheels, in conjunction with axles, allow heavy objects to be moved easily, facilitating transportation, supporting loads, and performing work in machines. Wheels are also used for other purposes, such as a ship's wheel, steering wheel, potter's wheel, and flywheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_Kelten_011.4.jpg/330px-Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_Kelten_011.4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheel","wikidata":"Q446"},{"title":"Color of water","blocks":[{"text":"The color of water varies with the ambient conditions in which that water is present. While relatively small quantities of water appear to be colorless and transparent, pure water has a slight blue color that becomes deeper as the thickness of the observed sample increases. The hue of water is an intrinsic property and is caused by selective absorption and scattering of blue light. Dissolved elements or suspended impurities may give water a different color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG/330px-Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_of_water","wikidata":"Q5148636"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/De_re_metallica_-_water_wheel_%28125711501%29.jpg/500px-De_re_metallica_-_water_wheel_%28125711501%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/De_re_metallica_-_water_wheel_%28125711501%29.jpg/1280px-De_re_metallica_-_water_wheel_%28125711501%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/De_re_metallica_-_water_wheel_%28125711501%29.jpg/500px-De_re_metallica_-_water_wheel_%28125711501%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The reversible water wheel powering a mine hoist in De re metallica (Georgius Agricola, 1566)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADe_re_metallica_-_water_wheel_(125711501).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Waterwheel_that_used_to_crush_grain_into_flour.jpg/500px-Waterwheel_that_used_to_crush_grain_into_flour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Waterwheel_that_used_to_crush_grain_into_flour.jpg/1280px-Waterwheel_that_used_to_crush_grain_into_flour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Waterwheel_that_used_to_crush_grain_into_flour.jpg/500px-Waterwheel_that_used_to_crush_grain_into_flour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A vertical water wheel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWaterwheel_that_used_to_crush_grain_into_flour.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Finch_Foundary_Water_Wheel%2C_Devon%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/500px-Finch_Foundary_Water_Wheel%2C_Devon%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Finch_Foundary_Water_Wheel%2C_Devon%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/1280px-Finch_Foundary_Water_Wheel%2C_Devon%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Finch_Foundary_Water_Wheel%2C_Devon%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/500px-Finch_Foundary_Water_Wheel%2C_Devon%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of Finch Foundry's water wheels.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFinch_Foundary_Water_Wheel%2C_Devon%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/AndersonMill.jpg/500px-AndersonMill.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/AndersonMill.jpg/500px-AndersonMill.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Anderson Mill of Texas is undershot, backshot, and overshot using two sources of water. This allows the direction of the wheel to be reversed.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAndersonMill.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Tiangong_Kaiwu_Chain_Pumps.jpg/250px-Tiangong_Kaiwu_Chain_Pumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Tiangong_Kaiwu_Chain_Pumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Tiangong_Kaiwu_Chain_Pumps.jpg/250px-Tiangong_Kaiwu_Chain_Pumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two types of hydraulic-powered chain pumps from the Tiangong Kaiwu of 1637, written by the Ming Dynasty encyclopedist, Song Yingxing (1587–1666).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATiangong_Kaiwu_Chain_Pumps.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/WaterwheelsSp.jpg/500px-WaterwheelsSp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikiped